<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhône</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rhône is a major river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 810 kilometres long. It rises in the Rhône Glacier in the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, and reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in a wide delta in the Camargue. In antiquity it carried trade from the Greek colony of Massalia into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and many Gallic peoples, from the Allobroges to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a>, lived along its valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhone.jpg" alt="Rhône" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benoît Prieur · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_du_Rh%C3%B4ne_depuis_le_Pont_Wilson_(Lyon),_mai_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
